  "summary": "# Daily Health-Tech Digest – November 5, 2026\n\nThe health-tech landscape is shifting as structural changes and political dynamics reshape the industry. CVS Health announced plans to split into two separate companies by 2028, though experts warn the separation may not fully resolve the conflicts of interest between its insurance and pharmacy benefit management operations. Meanwhile, the midterm elections have positioned Democrats with new congressional power over health policy, with supporters of Medicare for All set to chair key committees and clinical leaders gaining influence over FDA funding and regulatory agendas.\n\nOn the innovation front, researchers at Mount Sinai received a $4 million NIH grant to develop AI models predicting acute kidney injury risk during surgery, while a Taipei hospital is pursuing similar work. Regulators continue flexing enforcement muscles, with the FTC sending warning letters to health plan marketers over deceptive practices and Medicare testing artificial intelligence for prior authorization decisions—though experts question whether these AI experiments are ready for deployment. Recent setbacks in biotech included Servier's muscular dystrophy drug failing a pivotal trial and Ultragenyx cutting 20 percent of staff after additional drug development failures, while other companies like Inovio, MMI, and Bambusa are securing approvals and funding to advance new treatments in immunotherapy, microsurgery, and immunology respectively.",
